ArrayList:
- ArrayList is a resizing array that can grow and shrink dynamically as more items are added or removed.
- The class of ArrayList is part of the .NET System.Collections namespace.
- ArrayList has dynamic resizing ability. When you insert an item into it, if your array is full, your .NET runtime will create a new, larger Array and copy the items from the old one to the new one. 
- Removing an item from an ArrayList will not actually discard the item — it will instead copy all the items after the removed one into the new array that it creates.
- ArrayLists are a type of collection that can hold duplicate values and are very flexible. You can add, access, remove, or search for elements in an ArrayList.


ArrayList Class Methods: 
- Add(): The Add method adds an item to the end of the ArrayList. 
- Remove(): The Remove method deletes the first occurrence of a specified value. The method returns false if the item is not found.
- Insert(): The Insert method inserts an item at a specific index in the ArrayList.
- IndexOf(): The IndexOf method returns the index of the first occurrence of a specific value.
- LastIndexOf(): The LastIndexOf method returns the index of the last occurrence of a specific value. 
- Contains(): The Contains method determines whether an item is present in the ArrayList.

This is the contract of ArrayList class. 
I have written three test methods using [PexMethod] attribute which are: 
1. `PUT_AddContract` : tests the Add function 
2. `PUT_RemoveContract` : tests the Remove function 
3. `PUT_InsertContract` : tests the Insert function using the assumptions for input, i.e., index has to be within the valid range.

Each function tests with two values, Old which are values before execution and New which are after execution to observe any changes. 
In `PUT_InsertContract`, I also added an Assert for @@@ position of x in the old array is more than or equal to -1.

The `PUT_InsertContract` also makes sure that the values of `Old_arrListIndexOf_x` and `Old_arrListLastIndexOf_x` are -1 as these two are relevant to the case when element is not found during the search. 

Please note that, due to some limitations with testing complex objects like ArrayList, I am not able to use Pex on these contracts right now.

Also notice that this class contract testing do not really show the power of Pex as it only tests basic functionalities (Add/Remove/Insert). If you would like to test more complex method like the sort function or to exercise all edge cases, then Pex would be a great tool.

// This is an experimental code and may not work perfectly for all the test cases. Its design is meant to serve as a basic and simplistic contract testing for ArrayList class. It will need to be refined and enhanced based on specific requirements and expected behaviour.

```


// Pex does not support array indexed properties.
// If you wish to put your Pex on arraylist, you will have to refactor your tests into a form that uses index-based operations that Pex can understand.

```


Microsoft.Pex (Program eXploration) is a unit test generation tool that specializes in exploring test cases to ensure code robustness. To use Pex, you write tests written as methods decorated with PexMethod attribute and use it to generate input values to a method.

// To Pex the Add method, the test method should be decorated with the PexMethod attribute and takes an input array list and a value to add. Here is an example:
```csharp
[PexMethod]
public void Pex_Test_Add_Method(ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    arrList.Add(x);
    PexAssert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));
}
```
The Pex_Test_Add_Method takes an ArrayList and an integer as a parameter. It adds the integer to the ArrayList using the Add method and then checks if the ArrayList contains the integer using the Contains method. If the integer is successfully added to the ArrayList, then PexAssert.IsTrue will pass and Pex will generate a new set of input for the next run.

To Pex the Remove method, the test method should also be decorated with the PexMethod attribute and takes an input array list and a value to remove. Here is an example:
```csharp
[PexMethod--------------------            Debug.Assert((Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1) || (Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >=-1));

        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_ClearContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            bool Old_ArrListIsReadOnly = arrList.IsReadOnly;

            arrList.Clear();

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            bool New_ArrListIsReadOnly = arrList.IsReadOnly;

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IsReadOnly", Old_ArrListIsReadOnly);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IsReadOnly", New_ArrListIsReadOnly);



            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ @@@@@@ is equal to 0 
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == 0);

        }
    }
}


}


I am learning to perform unit testing and want to test this code. I only want to test these four methods: Add, Remove, Insert, Clear. I used Pex to create initial data for the test. Now I would like to verify or check the output of these methods using the Pex method decorator. But I can't seem to find any material that can guide me through how to do this. May someone help me to understand how to do it?
This is a simple and clear example of unit testing using Pex to check the methods of ArrayList (a simple array list implementation). I have written a simple test by using the Pex method decorator that has no output. I am not sure how to check or verify the result of these methods.

A: The output verification in the Pex framework or any other framework is not a feature of the framework itself. Instead, such verification is usually done manually or with some custom scripts or custom asserts. Usually, these results are compared with expected results which are known beforehand.

When you use Pex, your actual testing starts within a "Contract Test" framework such as those provided by Visual Studio. These Contract Test methods have contracts (a predictable/known outcome) set by the test framework. They then create some inputs but don't actually verify anything yet when they run the code; the verification part is left to your automated testing tool such as Pex, which just collects its data and compares it with the pre-set contracts.

In your case, since you're using Pex to test the ArrayList class, you should write separate tests in another framework (like NUnit, Xunit) that will use the ArrayList class as inputs and compare its behavior with the known expected behaviors. These tests will be more focused and less prone to errors by providing expected inputs and asserting what the expected outcome should be.

Here is a general guideline how you might start to write such tests:

1. First, you know what inputs will cause some possible problem/bug. You can set these as your tests inputs to ensure that the problem gets highlighted.

2. You make sure your ArrayList implementation behaves according to specification. The specification might include whether a call to Add should add an item to the list, what the Count property is after clearing the list, and so on.

3. You use the NUnit or other Assert methods to compare the expected result with the actual result before you continue. 

One important thing to remember when writing these tests is to always assume that every possible input could be provided, and therefore you need to handle any potential exceptions that might be thrown to maintain robust tests.

